{#
  Migrations 0052 / 0053 — DECLARED SCOPE, PINNED TO AN EXACT CLAUSE VERSION.
  ARCHITECTURE.md §5.5 (verbatim); §16 MI05, MI30; spec R-3.

  These two tables are what a subject SAYS it touches. They are claims, not derivations,
  and the schema keeps them typed as claims: nothing in the gate reads `relation` as a
  derived dependency edge (I06 reserves that for relations a projector computes). What
  the gate does read is the CONSEQUENCE of the claim — the exposure slice bounds which
  clauses may raise a blocking check, and the published denominator for every rate the
  vertical quotes is that bounded set rather than "every clause in the corpus".

  ── THE COMPOSITE VERSION KEY IS THE LOAD-BEARING PART ────────────────────────────
  The foreign key is onto `(clause_uuid, commit_id)` and NOT onto `clause_uuid` alone.
  Naming the version is what makes a declared scope un-inheritable across a revision: when
  the clause is re-authored, the pair changes, so a row that pointed at the old pair does
  not silently follow the clause forward into text nobody read. The same discipline on
  the obligation table (`fk_check_version`, conformance case CF-42) is what stops a
  disposition being inherited across a clause revision; here it is what stops a permit's
  DECLARED scope drifting under it while the permit is open.

  `commit_id` is a content address — sha256 over the canonical commit envelope, 32 bytes —
  never a counter, because the repository has no generator anywhere in it and a gap in a
  content-addressed chain is not a gap, it is a different object.

  ── TWO VOCABULARIES, AND WHY THEY DIFFER ─────────────────────────────────────────
  A permit RELATES to clauses it works under: it waives one, weakens another, relies on a
  third, cites a fourth. A change request relates to clauses it CHANGES: edits,
  introduces, retires. Sharing one vocabulary across both would make `weakens` legal on a
  document branch and `introduces` legal on a work permit, and the blame projection reads
  these tokens — a permit that claimed to `introduce` a clause would be asserting
  authorship it does not have.

  Both vocabularies are closed CHECKs rather than enum types, because they are the only
  place in this file where a vertical could conceivably need a wider set, and a CHECK is
  amendable in one ALTER while an enum in the substrate would be a MAJOR spec change.

  ── THE ONE VERTICAL RELATION THE SUBSTRATE DOES NAME ─────────────────────────────
  `<schema>.clause_version (clause_uuid, commit_id)` is a REQUIRED relation of any
  TRAPPOINT binding: the obligation table names it too (`fk_check_version`), and the
  reference vertical carries a hand-written stub for exactly this reason. It is named here
  in full knowledge of the MR-1 seam rule, and the justification is that both bindings
  create it and the conformance corpus asserts against it. `ref`, by contrast, is named by
  NO binding but MAINLINE, which is why the subject tables carry no foreign key to it.
#}
{#
  Per-kind constants the render context does not carry. `clause_spec` is keyed on subject
  KIND, so a binding that declares a kind the kernel has no migration number for is a
  render refusal (a KeyError reported against this template) rather than a file that
  silently does not exist. The migration numbers are §18 / MR-8 anchors and the constraint
  names satisfy R-3 exhibit uniqueness — `fk_version` on two tables would make "refused by
  fk_version" a sentence that needs a footnote.
#}
{% set clause_spec = {
     'permit': {
       'file':      '0052_permit_clause.sql',
       'table':     'permit_clause',
       'pk':        'pk_permit_clause',
       'fk_subject':'fk_permit_clause_subject',
       'fk_version':'fk_permit_clause_version',
       'vocab':     'permit_clause_relation_known',
       'sized':     'permit_clause_commit_sized',
       'relations': ['waives', 'weakens', 'relies_on', 'cites'],
       'mi':        ['MI05'],
       'gloss':     'what the crew works UNDER',
     },
     'change_request': {
       'file':      '0053_cr_clause.sql',
       'table':     'cr_clause',
       'pk':        'pk_cr_clause',
       'fk_subject':'fk_cr_clause_subject',
       'fk_version':'fk_cr_clause_version',
       'vocab':     'cr_clause_relation_known',
       'sized':     'cr_clause_commit_sized',
       'relations': ['edits', 'introduces', 'retires'],
       'mi':        ['MI30'],
       'gloss':     'what the change request CHANGES',
     },
   } %}
{% for s in subjects %}
{% set spec = clause_spec[s.kind] %}
-- @file {{ spec.file }}
{{ header(file=spec.file,
          title='CREATE TABLE ' ~ binding.schema ~ '.' ~ spec.table ~ ' — declared scope, pinned to an exact clause version',
          rationale='Declared scope is a claim and is stored as one; what the gate reads is its consequence. The foreign key is onto the (clause_uuid, commit_id) PAIR rather than onto the clause, so a re-authored clause does not silently carry an old declaration forward into text nobody read — the same discipline that makes a disposition un-inheritable across a revision. The relation vocabulary is closed and differs by subject kind because a work permit that claimed to introduce a clause would be asserting an authorship it does not have.',
          mi=spec.mi, i=['I02']) }}
--
-- {{ spec.table }}: {{ spec.gloss }}.
--
-- PRIMARY KEY ({{ s.id_column }}, clause_uuid, relation)
--   `relation` is IN the key, not beside it. One subject may both weaken a clause and
--   rely on it, and those are two different declarations with two different consequences
--   for the slice; collapsing them onto one row would make the second overwrite the
--   first and the slice would then be computed from a claim nobody made.
--
-- NO ON DELETE CASCADE on either foreign key. A cascade rewrites history: deleting a
-- subject would silently erase what it declared it touched, which is the single most
-- useful row in a post-incident reconstruction. Deletes are revoked at the grant layer
-- as well; RESTRICT is the structural half that survives a grant being edited.

CREATE TABLE {{ binding.schema }}.{{ spec.table }} (
  {{ '%-12s'|format(s.id_column) }} UUID   NOT NULL,
  {{ '%-12s'|format('clause_uuid') }} UUID   NOT NULL,
  {{ '%-12s'|format('commit_id') }} BYTES  NOT NULL,
  {{ '%-12s'|format('relation') }} STRING NOT NULL,
  CONSTRAINT {{ spec.vocab }}
    CHECK (relation IN ({% for r in spec.relations %}'{{ r }}'{{ ', ' if not loop.last }}{% endfor %})),
  CONSTRAINT {{ spec.sized }} CHECK (length(commit_id) = 32),
  CONSTRAINT {{ spec.fk_subject }} FOREIGN KEY ({{ s.id_column }})
    REFERENCES {{ binding.schema }}.{{ s.table }} ({{ s.id_column }})
    ON UPDATE RESTRICT ON DELETE RESTRICT,
  CONSTRAINT {{ spec.fk_version }} FOREIGN KEY (clause_uuid, commit_id)
    REFERENCES {{ binding.schema }}.clause_version (clause_uuid, commit_id)
    ON UPDATE RESTRICT ON DELETE RESTRICT,
  CONSTRAINT {{ spec.pk }} PRIMARY KEY ({{ s.id_column }}, clause_uuid, relation)
);
{% endfor %}
